    "claim": "Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of truth, but as a non-inductive methodological rule grounded in the objective logic of testing: we prefer the theory that has survived the most severe tests because it has exposed itself to the greatest risk of falsification and failed to be eliminated, thereby offering the best available opportunity for future error detection. This preference does not smuggle in induction because it makes no probabilistic claim about the future; it is a strategic choice to maximize the growth of knowledge by retaining the most informative (and thus most testable) survivor. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of any guarantee that science converges toward truth or that acting on the best-corroborated theory is 'rational' in the sense of maximizing expected utility; it is rational only in the negative sense of minimizing the chance of clinging to a readily falsifiable error.",
